WEBVTT

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:03.700
Mindwtr is built for Getting Things Done.

00:00:03.867 --> 00:00:08.367
Your Inbox, projects, Weekly Review and
Focus are all part of that workflow.

00:00:08.533 --> 00:00:09.600
Here’s how to use them.

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:12.333
Everything goes into the Inbox first.

00:00:12.433 --> 00:00:16.733
Process each item into a next action, a
project, something you’re waiting for,

00:00:16.867 --> 00:00:18.233
reference, or someday.

00:00:18.333 --> 00:00:20.400
A project needs a next action.

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:24.533
Review those lists each week, then
star a few actions for today’s Focus.

00:00:24.567 --> 00:00:25.867
Now let’s do that in the app.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:30.600
Open Quick Add and write
down what’s on your mind.

00:00:30.933 --> 00:00:33.967
Here, we’re capturing a launch
checklist and giving it a due date.

00:00:34.167 --> 00:00:37.267
Save it, and it goes into
your Inbox to sort out later.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:41.733
Open your Inbox and choose Process Inbox.

00:00:41.833 --> 00:00:43.533
You can work through one item at a time.

00:00:43.733 --> 00:00:47.467
In the guided view, Mindwtr asks whether
there’s something you need to do about it.

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:49.333
If there is, choose Yes.

00:00:49.367 --> 00:00:51.767
Then decide whether it’s
one action or a project.

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:57.700
For something you just want to keep, like
these presentation slides, choose Reference.

00:00:57.967 --> 00:01:00.800
A photography course you’re
considering can go in Someday.

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:03.300
And an expired flyer can go in Trash.

00:01:03.533 --> 00:01:07.700
Each decision clears an item from your
Inbox, so you can move on to the next one.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:12.233
Here’s a different example:
launching a community campaign.

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:16.200
We’ll make it a project, with drafting
the campaign brief as the first action.

00:01:16.267 --> 00:01:18.800
We can add a kickoff meeting too, then save.

00:01:18.933 --> 00:01:21.200
Open the project and you’ll see both items.

00:01:21.367 --> 00:01:26.000
The first is a Next Action; the extra
item stays in Inbox until you clarify it.

00:01:26.167 --> 00:01:28.600
You now have a project
and somewhere to begin.

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:33.733
For this item, we need a reply from Marcus.

00:01:33.967 --> 00:01:35.633
Choose Delegate and add his name.

00:01:35.733 --> 00:01:38.933
We can set a follow-up for next
week, then save it to Waiting For.

00:01:39.133 --> 00:01:42.033
That gives us somewhere to check
when it’s time to follow up.

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:48.567
If the next step only takes a couple of
minutes, you can do it now and mark it done.

00:01:48.767 --> 00:01:50.933
Otherwise, keep it as a next action.

00:01:51.133 --> 00:01:55.400
A context, like calls, helps you find it
when you’re ready to do that kind of work.

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:59.367
Your plans will change, so make
some time for a weekly review.

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:01.300
Open Review and follow the guide.

00:02:01.467 --> 00:02:04.467
Check your Inbox and calendar,
then look through your projects.

00:02:04.633 --> 00:02:06.567
Is there a next action for each one?

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:10.633
Check who you’re waiting for, and whether
any someday ideas are ready to move forward.

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:13.633
You’re bringing the list up to
date so you can trust it again.

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:19.067
Now let’s choose something to work on.

00:02:19.167 --> 00:02:21.500
Here, we’ll star the reply to the accountant.

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:23.433
It moves into Today’s Focus.

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:27.467
You can fold the other sections and keep
the few actions you’ve chosen in view.

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:31.767
Pick something that fits the time and
energy you have, and give it your attention.

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:36.067
Start with a few things in your Inbox.

00:02:36.133 --> 00:02:39.433
Work through one, choose its
next action, and give it a try.
